---
import LegalLayout from "../../layouts/LegalLayout.astro";
import { BRAND } from "@vc/config";
import { legalUpdatedLabel } from "../../lib/legal-pages";
import { isMarketingHost } from "../../server/public-blog";
import { publicRuntimeEnv } from "../../server/runtime";

const env = publicRuntimeEnv(Astro);
if (env.selfHosted || !isMarketingHost(Astro.request, env)) {
  return new Response("Not found", {
    status: 404,
    headers: { "content-type": "text/plain; charset=utf-8" },
  });
}
---
<LegalLayout
  title="Privacy Policy"
  description={`How ${BRAND.name} collects, uses, and protects your data.`}
  updated={legalUpdatedLabel("privacy")}
>
  <h2>The short version</h2>
  <p>
    We collect only what it takes to run your blog: your sign-in email, your content, and the
    minimum technical data to keep the service secure and reliable. We do not sell data, we do not
    run ads, and your posts belong to you. Reader analytics on hosted blogs use no cookies and store
    no visitor identifiers.
  </p>

  <h2>What we collect</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Account.</strong> The email address you sign in with, used to send one-time sign-in codes. When Google or GitHub sign-in is configured and you use it, we store what that provider returns to sign you in and link the account to your email: your account ID there, your name and profile image, and the sign-in tokens it issues.</li>
    <li><strong>Sessions.</strong> Signing in to the app sets an essential session cookie. Each session records the IP address and browser user agent it started from.</li>
    <li><strong>Workspace content.</strong> Your blog&rsquo;s posts, media, settings, version history, and activity log.</li>
    <li><strong>Access tokens.</strong> API and MCP tokens are stored only as hashes. Full tokens are shown once at creation.</li>
    <li><strong>Billing.</strong> Payments are processed by Polar. At checkout we pass Polar your email, name, and workspace ID. We never see or store your card number.</li>
    <li><strong>Operations.</strong> Request logs from our hosting provider for security and debugging.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What we never do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Sell, rent, or broker your data.</li>
    <li>Load advertising networks or third-party trackers on the app or marketing site.</li>
    <li>Read your content to train models. Agents you authorize access it over scoped tokens; their changes appear in your activity log.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Reader analytics on your blog</h2>
  <p>
    Hosted blogs count page views so you can see what resonates. A page view records the site, the
    post, and the hostname of the referring site. It stores no IP address, cookie, or visitor
    identifier. Browsers sending Do Not Track or Global Privacy Control are not counted.
  </p>
  <p>
    Reads by AI agents and crawlers are counted separately, on the server. Each count records the
    agent name or category from the user agent, the page or feed requested and its format (HTML,
    Markdown, XML, or text), whether a Signature-Agent header is present, and the blog&rsquo;s hostname. Requests for
    feeds, the sitemap, robots.txt, llms.txt, and Markdown versions of posts count here whoever
    makes them. This count stores no IP address or reader identifier, so it does not check Do Not
    Track or Global Privacy Control.
  </p>
  <p>
    Newsletter signups are opt-in. We store the email address, the page it came from, the consent
    wording shown, and keyed hashes of the IP address and user agent. Blogs set no cookies; reader
    preferences such as light or dark mode stay in the reader&rsquo;s own browser storage.
  </p>

  <h2>AI review</h2>
  <p>
    AI SEO review is opt-in. Only when you or your agent asks for one, we send the site name and the
    post&rsquo;s title, description, focus keyword, and up to 24,000 characters of its Markdown to
    Cloudflare Workers AI. This includes unpublished drafts. Results are cached by post version and
    model.
  </p>

  <h2>Providers</h2>
  <p>
    The service runs on Cloudflare: compute, database, and media storage, Cloudflare Email Sending
    for sign-in codes, and Workers AI for requested reviews. Polar processes payments. Each
    provider processes data only as needed to deliver the service.
  </p>

  <h2>Your content and your exits</h2>
  <p>
    You own everything you publish. Your posts and media are readable back through the same typed
    API and CLI your agents use, so leaving is a read operation, not a negotiation. Ask us to
    delete a workspace and we remove its content. Our database provider&rsquo;s point-in-time
    recovery can hold earlier copies until they age out.
  </p>

  <h2>Early access</h2>
  <p>
    vibecms is in public early access, and this policy will mature with the product. If a change
    materially expands what we collect, we will announce it on the site before it takes effect.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    Questions or requests about your data: <a href="mailto:support@vibecms.dev">support@vibecms.dev</a>.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
